  "analysis:diagTitleE": "Distortion at each Signal Level",
  "analysis:diagTitleT": "2nd harmonic of single tones",
  "analysis:diagTitleT3": "3rd harmonic of single tones",
  "analysis:diagTitleH": "Harmonic distortion",
  "analysis:diagTitleB": "Noise during playback",
  "analysis:diagTitleA": "Harmonic change at 6 dB lower Signal Level",
  "analysis:diagTitleC": "Harmonic orders",
  "analysis:diagTitleD": "Gain change with Signal Level",
  "analysis:diagTitleS": "Static distortion at the wave peaks",
  "analysis:diagTitleSum": "Summary",
  "analysis:diagCaptionE": "Each solid line is the distortion measured at one Signal Level, at the microphone position; lower is better. The dashed line was measured in the correction check with the correction applied. Dotted lines are predicted. The grey area is below the noise floor.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionT": "The 2nd harmonic of a single tone at each frequency, at the set Signal Level; lower is better. The solid line was measured without the correction. Dotted lines show what to expect with the correction; where a dotted line is above the solid line, the correction would add distortion to single tones. The grey area is below the noise floor.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionT3": "The 3rd harmonic of a single tone at each frequency, at the set Signal Level; lower is better. The solid line was measured without the correction. The dotted line shows what to expect with the odd-order correction; where it is above the solid line, the odd-order correction would add distortion to single tones. The grey area is below the noise floor.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionH": "Total harmonic distortion (THD, the 2nd to the 10th harmonic) and single harmonics of the speaker without the correction, at the set Signal Level; lower is better. The thin dashed line is THD at 6 dB lower Signal Level.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionB": "Sound that is not a harmonic of the test signal, such as a rattle, a buzz or room noise. A rise above the dashed line points to something loose or buzzing.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionA": "When the test signal is 6 dB lower, distortion this correction can reduce drops by about 6 dB (dashed line). Distortion that drops much more is of a kind it cannot reduce.",
  "analysis:diagOverloadNoteA": "Volume too high at {span}: the distortion that falls far below the dashed line here is caused by the sound being cut off at the limit, and cannot be corrected.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionC": "Level of each harmonic at the set Signal Level. The correction mainly lowers the 2nd harmonic. Odd orders (3rd, 5th) are lowered only below 2 kHz, and only when the correction check measured a benefit.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionD": "The speaker's gain at the set Signal Level, compared with 6 dB lower. A dip means the speaker or the amplifier is compressing at the set Signal Level.",
  "analysis:diagOverloadNoteD": "Volume too high at {span}: turning the Signal Level up no longer makes the sound that much louder.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionS": "The part of the 2nd and 3rd harmonic distortion that acts like a fixed input–output curve, shown as the gain at the positive and the negative peak of a single tone at the set Signal Level, compared with a quiet tone. Below 0 dB the peak is squashed, above 0 dB it is stretched; lines that split apart mean the two halves of the wave are treated differently. The dotted lines show where the solid lines would be if all of this distortion were of that kind. Inside the grey area the difference from 0 dB is below the noise floor.",
  "analysis:diagCaptionSMap": "Each column shows how a single tone at that frequency passes through the speaker at the set Signal Level: the colour is the gain at each instantaneous level of the wave, compared with a quiet tone, blue where the wave is squashed and orange where it is stretched. The top and bottom edges are the positive and the negative peak. The levels in between are calculated from the 2nd and 3rd harmonics, not measured. A column without colour has no static distortion above the noise floor.",
