  "analysis:diagR2Obs": "Level-invariant distortion: {list} changes by less than 2 dB at 6 dB lower Signal Level ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR2Cause": "Likely hysteresis in the speaker's magnet system, an iron-core crossover coil or the output filter of a small class-D amplifier, friction, a limiter or DSP.",
  "analysis:diagR2Action": "Playing more quietly will not reduce it. Check the DSP and limiter settings, or measure with another amplifier and compare the two measurements.",
  "analysis:diagR3Obs": "Distortion rising at low level: {list} rises by at least 2 dB at 6 dB lower Signal Level ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR3Cause": "Likely crossover or dead-time distortion in the amplifier's output stage, or friction.",
  "analysis:diagR3Action": "Try another amplifier or output, then compare the two measurements.",
  "analysis:diagR4Obs": "Sudden onset: {k} harmonics are at least 10 dB stronger at the set Signal Level than at 6 dB lower, at {span} ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR4Cause": "Likely clipping, the cone reaching the end of its travel, or a limiter.",
  "analysis:diagR4Action": "Listen more quietly. This distortion starts between the set Signal Level and 6 dB lower, around {span}.",
  "analysis:diagR5Obs": "Rattle around {span} ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR5Action": "Something loose in or near the speaker, such as a panel, the grille or an object on the shelf, may be buzzing. Listen for a rattle around {span} while a test signal plays, and fix or remove its source.",
  "button:excludeRattle": "Correct only below {f} (avoids the rattle)",
  "analysis:rattleOutside": "It is already above the Upper Limit.",
  "analysis:diagR6Obs": "Hum: {f} Hz is {e} dB above the neighbouring noise in silence ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR6Cause": "Likely a ground loop or hum from a power supply.",
  "analysis:diagR6Action": "Check the ground connection, plug all devices into one power strip, or try a different cable route.",
  "analysis:diagOverloadObs": "Volume too high: at the set Signal Level, the sound at {span} is up to {m} dB quieter than it should be ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagOverloadCause": "The amplifier or the speaker has reached the loudest it can play, so the sound is cut off at that limit and distorts.",
  "analysis:diagOverloadAction": "Turn down the amplifier volume or lower the Signal Level, then measure again. This distortion cannot be corrected; the correction works only at volumes below this limit.",
  "analysis:diagR7Obs": "Gain drops by at least 0.3 dB at the set Signal Level across 200 Hz – 8 kHz ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR7Cause": "Likely the amplifier's power supply sagging, or a limiter.",
  "analysis:diagR7Action": "Listen more quietly or use a more powerful amplifier.",
  "analysis:diagR8Obs": "Bass compression: the gain at {span} drops by {m} dB at the set Signal Level ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR8Cause": "Likely the cone or the port reaching its limit.",
  "analysis:diagR8Action": "Lower the bass level, add a high-pass filter for this speaker, or let a subwoofer play below about {f} Hz.",
  "analysis:diagR9Obs": "Asymmetry: even-order distortion is at least 6 dB above odd-order at {span} ({chart}).",
  "analysis:diagR9Cause": "Likely a voice-coil offset, suspension asymmetry, or DC at the amplifier output.",
  "analysis:diagR9ActionOn": "Turn on the corrector (the first item gives the expected reduction).",
  "analysis:diagR9ActionOff": "Check for DC at the amplifier output.",
  "analysis:diagNotEvalObs": "Not evaluable: not enough signal above the noise floor at {span}.",
  "analysis:diagNotEvalAction": "Move the microphone closer to the speaker (no closer than 0.5 m), raise the Signal Level or increase Distortion Averaging, then measure again.",
  "analysis:diagReadingNote": "These are likely explanations from the patterns in the charts, not a certain diagnosis.",
  // End of Diagnosis tabs
  // Speaker distortion measurement screen
  "title:newMeasurement": "New measurement",
  "title:frequencyResponseChoice": "Frequency response",
  "message:frequencyResponseChoice": "Measures frequency response, impulse response and harmonic distortion with sweeps. Use it for Room EQ and the other corrections.",
  "button:measureFrequencyResponse": "Measure frequency response",
  "title:speakerDistortionChoice": "Speaker distortion",
  "message:speakerDistortionChoice": "Measures how each speaker distorts at your listening volume and prepares the Distortion Correction effect. It measures distortion with music-like test signals at several levels and builds a correction from it. Many speakers cannot be improved by this correction; the result still shows what kind of distortion they have. Takes {time} per channel; the test signals are loud.",
  "button:measureSpeakerDistortion": "Measure speaker distortion",
  "title:measureSpeakerDistortion": "Measure speaker distortion",
  "label:stepSetup": "Setup",
  "label:stepLevelCheck": "Level check",
  "label:stepMeasure": "Measure",
  "label:channelsToMeasure": "Channels to measure:",
  "help:channelsToMeasure": "Each channel is measured on its own, one after another.",
  "message:speakerDistortionBandLimited": "The test signals are limited to {bands}, so the correction is made for a channel that plays only this range. To measure a full-range speaker, set Sweep Bandwidth to Off.",
  "message:speakerDistortionBandUnmeasurable": "Distortion cannot be measured with the current Sweep Bandwidth ({bands}): distortion is measured from 50 Hz to 8 kHz, and too little of that range is left. Widen Sweep Bandwidth, or clear the channel under Channels to measure.",
  "message:amplifierVolume": "Set the amplifier to the loudest volume you listen at. The correction is made for this amplifier volume. Afterwards it is best to change loudness inside EffeTune. If you turn the amplifier down later, run Check again so the correction follows. If you turn it up beyond this volume, measure again.",
  "label:microphonePosition": "Microphone position:",
  "option:inFront": "In front of the speaker (recommended)",
  "option:listeningPosition": "Listening position (no need to move the microphone; usually corrects less)",
  "help:microphonePosition": "The correction is computed from the speaker itself, which works best at most seats. The figures shown are for the microphone spot; at your seat the reduction is similar below about 2 kHz and usually smaller above.",
  "message:placeInFront1": "Place the microphone 0.5–1 m (20–40 in) in front of the speaker being measured, pointing at it.",
  "message:placeInFront2": "Set it at the height midway between the woofer and the tweeter (for a speaker with one driver, at its centre). Keep it away from table tops and walls.",
  "message:placeInFront3": "In a room with a lot of echo, use 0.5 m.",
  "help:placeInFrontWhy": "At this distance the microphone hears the woofer and tweeter in the same balance as you do. The closer the microphone, the less the room's echo matters. Very close to the woofer it hears mostly the woofer, and the correction becomes less accurate above the bass.",
  "message:placeListeningPosition": "Leave the microphone at your listening position, pointing at the speakers, for all channels.",
  "label:diagramSpeaker": "speaker",
  "label:diagramMicrophone": "microphone",
  "label:diagramDistance": "0.5–1 m",
  "label:distortionAveraging": "Distortion Averaging:",
  "status:perChannelTime": "{time} per channel",
  "help:distortionAveraging": "More averaging lowers the noise in the result but takes longer. Quick takes about half the time of ×2, but the correction is less accurate.",
  "label:distortionSignalLevel": "Signal Level:",
  "help:speakerDistortionSignalLevel": "Sets how loud the test signals are. Short loud peaks play 10 dB higher; at −10 dBFS they reach full scale. Lower it if the level check sounded strained. The correction still applies at your listening volume.",
  "label:retryOnNoise": "Retry on Noise:",
  "option:retryOnNoiseOn": "On",
  "option:retryOnNoiseOff": "Off",
  "help:retryOnNoise": "Replay a multitone test signal up to two more times if the recording is too noisy or unusable.",
  "label:durationMinute": "about a minute",
  "label:durationMinutes": "about {n} minutes",
  "message:speakerDistortionWaitForUpdate": "Wait for the result update to finish.",
  "message:speakerDistortionNeedsSampleRate": "This measurement needs a sample rate of 44.1 kHz or higher. Choose a higher sample rate for the audio device.",
  "title:beforeYouStart": "Before you start",
  "message:noticeTime": "After the level check, the measurement takes {total} for {n} channels ({each} each), including a short correction check at the end of each channel.",
  "message:noticeTimeOne": "After the level check, the measurement takes {total}, including a short correction check at the end.",
  "message:noticeLoud": "It is loud. The test signals play at the amplifier volume you set, and short peaks reach the full output level of your system.",
  "message:noticeLoudPeak": "It is loud. The test signals play at the amplifier volume you set, and short peaks reach {peak} dBFS.",
  "message:noticeLoudOdd": "When odd-order correction looks useful, about 20 more seconds play at high volume to confirm it does no harm there. This is the loudest sustained sound of the measurement.",
  "message:noticePeople": "If people or pets are nearby, let them know or ask them to leave the room. If you stay close to the speaker, ear protection is a good idea. Keep your neighbours in mind, too.",
  "message:noticeCancelIfNoise": "If you hear crackling, scraping or a hard knock from the speaker, press Cancel immediately.",
  "message:noticeSilence": "Keep the room as silent as you can until the measurement finishes. Any sound other than the test signal that reaches the microphone, such as voices, footsteps, fans, air conditioning or noise from outside, makes the result less accurate. Turn off what you can, close doors and windows, and stay still and quiet.",
  "message:noticeMicrophone": "Fix the microphone firmly on a stand so that it cannot move at all, and do not touch it, its stand or its cable until the measurement finishes. Do not hold it in your hand. A short level check runs before each channel.",
  "message:noticeMicrophoneInFront": "Fix the microphone firmly on a stand so that it cannot move at all while a channel is measured, and do not touch it, its stand or its cable. Do not hold it in your hand. Before each channel, you are asked to move it in front of that speaker, and a short level check runs.",
  "message:noticeExpectation": "Many speakers cannot be improved by this correction. Even then, the result shows what kind of distortion your speakers have.",
  "message:targetChannel": "Speaker distortion will be measured on {ch}.",
  "message:targetInFront": "Place the microphone 0.5–1 m (20–40 in) in front of that speaker, at the height midway between its woofer and tweeter.",
  "message:targetListeningPosition": "Leave the microphone at the listening position.",
  "button:runLevelCheck": "Run level check",
  "help:levelCheck": "Plays about 5 seconds of the test signals on the first channel, including the loudest one.",
  "status:levelChecking": "Checking the level…",
  "message:levelCheckOk": "Level check passed. The loudest test signal stayed about {db} dB below the microphone's limit. Signal-to-noise ratio: about {snr} dB.",
  "help:levelCheckLoudest": "The measurement's peaks go no higher than the short burst you just heard. If it was uncomfortable or the speaker sounded strained, go back and lower Signal Level.",
  "warning:levelCheckOverload": "The microphone input is overloaded. {advice}, then check again.",
  "advice:hotInFront": "Move the microphone back a little (it must stay within 1 m) or lower the microphone gain",
  "advice:hotListeningRun": "Lower the microphone gain",
  "advice:hotListeningSetup": "Lower the microphone gain or lower Signal Level",
  "warning:levelCheckQuiet": "The microphone picked up little or none of the test signal. Check that the right microphone and input channel are selected, that the microphone is not muted and that it points at the speaker, or raise the microphone gain, then check again.",
  "warning:levelCheckSnrGain": "The signal-to-noise ratio is low: correcting distortion of 1 % needs at least 40 dB. Raise the microphone gain by up to about {gain} dB or make the room quieter, then check again. You can also start the measurement as it is, but the correction will be less accurate.",
  "warning:levelCheckSnr": "The signal-to-noise ratio is low: correcting distortion of 1 % needs at least 40 dB. Make the room quieter, then check again. You can also start the measurement as it is, but the correction will be less accurate.",
